[Cheonji Ilbo Jinan=Reporter Kim Dong-hyun] Residents of Jinan County, North Jeolla Province, still have to take a 40&ndash;50 minute bus ride just to reach a hospital. The county&rsquo;s only hospital-level facility is Jinan Medical Center, the smallest among the nation&rsquo;s 35 local public hospitals. With 74 beds and about 130 staff, it runs 10 essential departments including internal medicine, surgery, and orthopedics, while keeping its emergency room lights on 24 hours a day. Locals say, &ldquo;It is reassuring just to have a nearby hospital without having to travel all the way to Jeonju.&rdquo;

Yet the shortage of specialists and limited facilities remain stark. With no delivery room, expectant mothers must go to nearby cities, and the absence of specialized departments forces residents to rely heavily on pharmacies. The lack of pharmacies open at night or on weekends adds to the difficulties during emergencies.

The county injects about 4 billion won (around USD 30 million) annually into running the center, but this remains insufficient. A medical center official said, &ldquo;The entire subsidy is used solely for personnel costs,&rdquo; stressing that &ldquo;legal and institutional support for operational funding is urgently needed.&rdquo; The small rural medical center sustaining essential healthcare underscores the dire need for greater national support.

Jinan Medical Center fills rural healthcare gaps

24-hour ER runs under staff shortages

County spends 3&ndash;4 billion KRW annually to sustain hospital

&nbsp;

No delivery room in obstetrics, mothers leave town

Specialist shortages force reliance on pharmacies

&ldquo;County funds limited&hellip; National support system needed&rdquo;



