[Cheonji Ilbo Jinan=Reporter Kim Dong-hyun] Donghyang-myeon, Jinan County, North Jeolla Province. A village bus winds down narrow mountain roads. Outside the window stretch endless rice paddies and fields, dotted with scattered farmhouses. A few elderly passengers with gray hair sit quietly. Some rest a small cloth bundle on their laps, others lean on walking sticks. Today again, they must travel 40&ndash;50 minutes just to visit a hospital in town.

On the bus, Ms. Lee (alias, in her 70s) softly remarked, &ldquo;I&rsquo;m just going to see the doctor.&rdquo; She added, &ldquo;Normally, I visit family medicine or Jinan Medical Center, but if something serious happens, I have no choice but to go to Jeonju. When buses don&rsquo;t run, I rely on my children&rsquo;s car, and it takes nearly two hours.&rdquo;

A visit to Jinan County reveals stark medical shortages. The county has only one hospital-level facility: Jinan Medical Center. It is the smallest of the 35 public hospitals nationwide, yet it means more than just a hospital to residents. It is the first place people rush to in emergencies, the only healthcare facility with &ldquo;lights on all night,&rdquo; and a lifeline for villagers.

◆Small but the only hospital

Jinan County&rsquo;s only hospital-level institution is Jinan Medical Center. With just 130 staff and 74 beds, it nevertheless operates 10 essential departments including internal medicine, surgery, orthopedics, pediatrics, obstetrics &amp; gynecology, and emergency medicine. It handles about 150 outpatients daily, while 5&ndash;10 patients visit the ER each day. For fever, abdominal pain, or heat stroke, this is where locals rush first.

A resident of Jinan-eup said, &ldquo;It takes a long drive to Jeonju, but just knowing the medical center is nearby is a relief. The fact that the lights are on even at night is the greatest comfort.&rdquo;

But the limits of its size are clear. The shortage of specialists means that only certain departments are staffed. Although there is an obstetrics &amp; gynecology clinic, there is no delivery room&mdash;forcing pregnant women to travel to nearby cities from prenatal checkups to delivery. Ultimately, residents must often rely on pharmacies or over-the-counter medicine.


