◆Residents turn to pharmacies

Mr. Kim (alias), a pharmacist in Jinan-eup, understands rural medical realities better than most. He said, &ldquo;Jinan Medical Center only has internal medicine, surgery, and neurology; dermatology isn&rsquo;t open daily, and there&rsquo;s no ENT. So residents often just buy common medicines like painkillers and digestive aids.&rdquo;

The gaps in healthcare are reflected in pharmacies. Ms. Kim (alias, in her 30s), who commutes daily from Jeonju to Jinan, said, &ldquo;Because there are no specialized departments, residents often come to the pharmacy asking for consultations. In cities, they would go straight to the hospital, but here they try to manage with medicine instead.&rdquo;

Another issue is the lack of late-night or 24-hour pharmacies. &ldquo;In cities, pharmacies are open at night or on weekends, but not in rural areas. So if someone gets sick on Saturday night or Sunday evening, they struggle to find medicine,&rdquo; Ms. Kim explained. Some regions provide subsidies by ordinance to extend pharmacy hours, but Jinan does not. She added, &ldquo;People ask us to stay open later, but without staff or support, it&rsquo;s not possible.&rdquo;


