◆&ldquo;Support is essential; survival has limits&rdquo;

Jinan County allocates around 4 billion KRW every year to keep the hospital running. Still, the county budget alone cannot sustain the entire operation. A 24-hour ER requires at least four specialists rotating shifts, plus radiology, nursing, and lab staff on standby. With salaries, equipment, and operating expenses combined, the financial strain is inevitable.

A Jinan Public Health Center official said, &ldquo;Essential healthcare should be the responsibility of the state and provincial governments. Expecting a county hospital to shoulder everything is unsustainable.&rdquo; During the COVID-19 pandemic, when Jinan Medical Center was designated as a dedicated hospital, general patients had to go elsewhere, and usage has not fully recovered since.

Another medical center representative noted, &ldquo;Doctor recruitment is the hardest challenge. Specialists avoid rural posts and prefer cities. Yet we have not suspended any departments. The 3&ndash;4 billion KRW from the county goes entirely to staff salaries, while maintenance and supplies must be covered by hospital revenue. Without local government support, survival would be nearly impossible. National support and legal institutionalization of operating subsidies for public hospitals are urgently needed.&rdquo;

For emergencies, it remains the first stop, the only place with &ldquo;lights on all night.&rdquo;

In this quiet mountain county, the small hospital sustaining rural lives poses a question to society: how will we prepare for the future of healthcare in farming regions?

